(b) /home/bblinn/fast_ws/scripts/parallel/run_job.sh -- FAILED-sentinel fix
    (OUTSIDE src/livo_recon; direct user request, not git-tracked; not meant
    to be `git apply`'d into this repository -- included for the record only)
===============================================================================
Direct user instruction that prompted this edit, quoted verbatim:
"can we fix the run_job.sh generic ATE line heuristic that marks jobs as
failed when evo lines aren't printed? the behavior should be that when a job
crashes or fails in some other way the script should note that it failed"

Problem: run_job.sh's livo_recon-branch DONE/FAILED decision required a
`[evo ATE=...]` line to appear in run.log. R72's new EF tier sets
`duration_secs = ceil(samples/197.5 + 8)` seconds (calibration length plus a
handful of dump frames only, by design) -- short enough that the node's own
`auto_terminate_idle_secs_` idle watchdog (src/livo_recon_node.cpp:517) ends
the run cleanly (exit_code=0, signal=none) before evo's scoring window
(onset_scan ~485+) is ever reached. The old heuristic could not distinguish
this normal, by-design early termination from an actual crash, and
misclassified all 40 of R72's EF arms as FAILED even though every one
completed exactly as designed with complete, correct raw output.

Fix: the real process exit status (`exit_status.txt`, already written just
before the DONE/FAILED decision) is now the authoritative crash signal.

--- a/scripts/parallel/run_job.sh (before, reconstructed)
+++ b/scripts/parallel/run_job.sh (after, as applied and verified live)
@@
-    if [[ "$last_evo" =~ $ATE_LINE_RE && -s "$OUT_DIR/odometry.txt" ]]; then
+    # R72: a job that exits cleanly (exit_code=0, no signal) but never prints
+    # an [evo ATE=...] line is not necessarily a failure -- a short/truncated
+    # run (e.g. gen_jobs.py's EF tier: duration_secs covers calibration plus
+    # a handful of dump frames, not the full bag) can legitimately end, via
+    # the node's own auto_terminate_idle_secs idle watchdog, well before evo
+    # reaches its scoring window (onset_scan ~485+). Before this fix,
+    # run_job.sh's generic ATE-line requirement misclassified every one of
+    # R72's 40 EF arms as FAILED even though all 40 completed exactly as
+    # designed with full, correct raw output -- confirmed by exit_status.txt
+    # reading exit_code=0 signal=none for all 40, with residual_dump.csv/
+    # plane_fit_trace.csv/state_trace.csv all present and complete. The real
+    # crash/failure signal is the actual process exit status (captured
+    # above), not whether evo happened to produce a line in time -- so a
+    # clean exit with the primary output (odometry.txt) present is DONE even
+    # without a matching [evo line; a crash (nonzero exit code or any
+    # signal) or missing/empty odometry.txt is still unconditionally FAILED.
+    _exit_status_text="$(cat "$OUT_DIR/exit_status.txt" 2>/dev/null)"
+    _clean_exit=0
+    [[ "$_exit_status_text" == "exit_code=0 signal=none" ]] && _clean_exit=1
+    if [[ ( "$last_evo" =~ $ATE_LINE_RE || "$_clean_exit" == "1" ) && -s "$OUT_DIR/odometry.txt" ]]; then
       ... (unchanged: the run.log-quiescence recheck, the write-then-rename
            of results_lio.txt)
-        echo "$last_evo" > "$_tmp_results"
+        if [[ "$last_evo" =~ $ATE_LINE_RE ]]; then
+          echo "$last_evo" > "$_tmp_results"
+        else
+          # R72: clean exit, no ATE -- see comment above the outer `if`.
+          echo "NO_ATE: clean exit ($_exit_status_text), odometry.txt present, no [evo ATE=...m line in run.log -- evo likely never reached its scoring window (a short/truncated-duration run, or evo disabled/misconfigured for this job); see run.log" > "$_tmp_results"
+        fi
         mv -f "$_tmp_results" "$JOB_DIR/results_lio.txt"
         touch "$JOB_DIR/DONE"
-        echo "[$(basename "$JOB_DIR")] $last_evo"
+        if [[ "$last_evo" =~ $ATE_LINE_RE ]]; then
+          echo "[$(basename "$JOB_DIR")] $last_evo"
+        else
+          echo "[$(basename "$JOB_DIR")] DONE ($_exit_status_text, no ATE line -- see results_lio.txt)"
+        fi
     else
       rm -f "$JOB_DIR/DONE"
       touch "$JOB_DIR/FAILED"
-      echo "[$(basename "$JOB_DIR")] FAILED (no ATE=...m [evo line in run.log after retries -- see $JOB_DIR/run.log)"
+      if [[ ! -s "$OUT_DIR/odometry.txt" ]]; then
+        echo "[$(basename "$JOB_DIR")] FAILED ($_exit_status_text -- \$OUT_DIR/odometry.txt missing or empty -- see $JOB_DIR/run.log)"
+      else
+        echo "[$(basename "$JOB_DIR")] FAILED ($_exit_status_text -- no ATE=...m [evo line in run.log after retries and the process did not exit cleanly -- last [evo line, if any: '${last_evo:-<none>}' -- see $JOB_DIR/run.log)"
+      fi
     fi

Net effect: DONE now fires if (ATE line matches OR the process exited
cleanly: exit_code=0/signal=none) AND odometry.txt is non-empty; otherwise
FAILED, and every FAILED message now always includes the real
`exit_status.txt` text so a future reader does not have to re-derive it. A
clean-exit-no-ATE DONE writes an explicit `NO_ATE: clean exit (...)` line
into `results_lio.txt` in place of a fabricated ATE line.

Verification this did not disturb the normal ATE-producing path: the edit
was applied mid-round, live, while `r72_sc` and `r72_co` dispatchers were
actively running. Immediately afterward: `r72_sc` completed 3/3 jobs with
real `[evo ATE=...]` lines; `r72_co` completed 10/10 jobs (including all 8
non-control gate-on arms, 3 of which crashed via SIGABRT and were correctly
still marked FAILED with the real signal text, and 5 of which produced real
ATE lines); `r72_p` subsequently completed 144/144 jobs, every one with a
real ATE line. Dispatch-log timestamps show continuous, uninterrupted
progress across the edit with no stalls or re-dispatches attributable to it.

