(c) /home/bblinn/fast_ws/scripts/parallel/run_job.sh -- R73 sentinel-masking fix
    (OUTSIDE src/livo_recon; direct user request, mid-round, same file as
    part (b) above but a SEPARATE, later edit; not meant to be `git apply`'d
    into this repository -- included for the record only)
===============================================================================
Direct user instruction that prompted this edit, quoted verbatim (in response
to the agent explaining the discovered bug mechanism):
"i don't think that's good logic. run job should report failed on crashes or
when we have to manually kill the process or something, this is unrelated to
whether or not we generated ATE data"

Problem: the DONE/FAILED gate (as left by the R72 fix, part (b) above) was
`(last_evo matches ATE_LINE_RE OR clean_exit) AND odometry.txt non-empty` --
an OR. A job that printed at least one periodic [evo/interp] diagnostic line
and THEN crashed still satisfied the ATE-line disjunct regardless of its
actual exit status, and was sentinelled DONE even though the process had
aborted. This went uncaught in R72 because evo there only ever logged once,
at the genuine end of a finished run -- "last_evo matches" was in practice a
reliable proxy for "reached the end normally." A long coupled job that logs
evo periodically throughout its run (rather than once at the end) breaks
that implicit assumption: a crash occurring any time after the first
periodic evaluation line is masked as DONE. Confirmed live this round on a
real crashed co-queue arm (SIGABRT, "joint-knot information matrix is not
positive definite") that had already logged a mid-run [evo/interp] line
before aborting.

Fix: the ATE-line disjunct was removed entirely from the gate.

--- a/scripts/parallel/run_job.sh (before, R72 state)
+++ b/scripts/parallel/run_job.sh (after, as applied and verified live)
@@
     _exit_status_text="$(cat "$OUT_DIR/exit_status.txt" 2>/dev/null)"
     _clean_exit=0
     [[ "$_exit_status_text" == "exit_code=0 signal=none" ]] && _clean_exit=1
-    if [[ ( "$last_evo" =~ $ATE_LINE_RE || "$_clean_exit" == "1" ) && -s "$OUT_DIR/odometry.txt" ]]; then
+    # R73 fix (direct user request): the DONE/FAILED decision must depend
+    # ONLY on how the process actually exited (clean vs crashed/killed),
+    # never on whether evo happened to produce a line -- ATE-line presence
+    # is relevant only to what gets WRITTEN into results_lio.txt (a real
+    # ATE line vs the NO_ATE placeholder), not to whether the job is DONE
+    # or FAILED. The previous `(ATE-line OR clean-exit)` OR let a job that
+    # printed a periodic (non-final) evo line and then crashed still read
+    # as DONE.
+    if [[ "$_clean_exit" == "1" && -s "$OUT_DIR/odometry.txt" ]]; then
       ... (unchanged: the two-sentinel-never-coexist clear, the
            run.log-quiescence recheck, the write-then-rename of
            results_lio.txt -- the ATE-line-vs-NO_ATE branching inside this
            block is UNCHANGED, still gated on `"$last_evo" =~ $ATE_LINE_RE`
            purely to decide what text to write, not whether DONE fires)
     else
       rm -f "$JOB_DIR/DONE"
       touch "$JOB_DIR/FAILED"
       if [[ ! -s "$OUT_DIR/odometry.txt" ]]; then
         echo "[$(basename "$JOB_DIR")] FAILED ($_exit_status_text -- \$OUT_DIR/odometry.txt missing or empty -- see $JOB_DIR/run.log)"
       else
-        echo "[$(basename "$JOB_DIR")] FAILED ($_exit_status_text -- no ATE=...m [evo line in run.log after retries and the process did not exit cleanly -- last [evo line, if any: '${last_evo:-<none>}' -- see $JOB_DIR/run.log)"
+        echo "[$(basename "$JOB_DIR")] FAILED ($_exit_status_text -- the process did not exit cleanly (crashed, was killed, or an unrecognized exit) -- last [evo line, if any: '${last_evo:-<none>}' -- see $JOB_DIR/run.log)"
       fi
     fi

Net effect: DONE now fires if and only if (exit_code=0 signal=none) AND
odometry.txt is non-empty. ATE-line presence never factors into DONE/FAILED
again, only into what results_lio.txt's content says. This also correctly
covers a manual/external kill: exit_status.txt's third possible value,
`exit_code=unknown signal=unknown` (neither a "process has died"-style death
nor a "process has finished cleanly" line in run.log -- e.g. an externally
applied kill that bypasses roslaunch's own exit reporting), also fails to
set _clean_exit=1 and correctly falls through to FAILED.

Verified: `bash -n scripts/parallel/run_job.sh` syntax-checks clean. Applied
mid-round while r73_p2's dispatcher was actively running; all of p2's
remaining ~95 jobs completed correctly afterward with real ATE lines,
confirming the fix did not disturb the normal ATE-producing path. Does NOT
retroactively re-sentinel jobs dispatched before this edit -- their on-disk
JOB_DIR sentinel remains whatever the pre-fix script wrote.
